//www.poynter.org/ifcn/2022/dubawa-fact-checker-ghana-democracy-building/ [Google Scholar]\n* 23.Kessler G. Trump made 30,573 false or misleading claims as president. Nearly half came in his final year. The Washington Post. 2021. Available: https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/how-fact-checker-tracked-trump-claims/2021/01/23/ad04b69a-5c1d-11eb-a976-bad6431e03e2\\_story.html [Google Scholar]\n* 24.Aird MJ, Ecker UKH, Swire B, Berinsky AJ, Lewandowsky S. Does truth matter to voters? The effects of correcting political misinformation in an Australian sample. Royal Society Open Science. 2018;5. doi: 10.1098/rsos.180593 [DOI] [PMC free article] [PubMed] [Google Scholar]\n* 25.Pennycook G, Epstein Z, Mosleh M, Arechar AA, Eckles D, Rand DG. Shifting attention to accuracy can reduce misinformation online. Nature. 2021;592: 590–595. doi: 10.1038/s41586-021-03344-2 [DOI] [PubMed] [Google Scholar]\n* 26.Graves L, Nyhan B, Riefler J. The diffusion of factchecking: Understanding the growth of a journalistic innovation. 2015. Available: http://www.americanpressinstitute.org/wp-content/uploads/2015/04/The-Growth-of-Fact-Checking.pdf [Google Scholar]\n* 27.Carpenter A. Gaslighting America: Why we love it when Trump lies to us. Broadside Books; 2018. [Google Scholar]\n* 28.Kessler G. Donald Trump and his assault on truth: The president’s falsehoods, misleading claims and flat-out lies. Scribner; 2020. [Google Scholar]\n* 29.Gallup. Presidential approval ratings—Donald Trump. Gallip. 2021. Available: https://news.gallup.com/poll/203198/presidential-approval-ratings-donald-trump.aspx [Google Scholar]\n* 30.Luo M, Hancock JT, Markowitz DM. Credibility perceptions and detection accuracy of fake news headlines on social media: Effects of truth-bias and endorsement cues. Communication Research. 2022;49: 171–195. doi: 10.1177/0093650220921321 [DOI] [Google Scholar]\n* 31.Pennycook G, Cannon TD, Rand DG. Prior exposure increases perceived accuracy of fake news. Journal of Experimental P